    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_715d01df",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "police_officers_talking",
      "label": "police officers sharing a joke",
      "description": "Two police officers are standing in a police station, smiling and looking at each other, suggesting they are sharing a humorous moment or joke. The text overlay indicates they are playfully pretending there are no donuts left when there are actually two.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_4b6ad958",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "workplace_banter",
      "label": "Workplace banter and camaraderie",
      "description": "The meme relies on the cultural understanding that colleagues, especially in close-knit or hierarchical environments like police departments, often engage in playful, mischievous, or deceptive banter. This is a common social phenomenon in many professions where shared humor and small deceptions (like pretending to run out of donuts) build rapport and create a sense of belonging. The humor is rooted in the relatability of such everyday workplace interactions.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7b9f12d6",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "donut_culture_in_workplaces",
      "label": "Donut culture in American workplaces",
      "description": "Donuts are a widely recognized and culturally embedded snack in American office and workplace settings, often associated with breaks, morale, and informal gatherings. The idea of 'running out of donuts' is a humorous trope that plays on the common experience of shared snacks in the workplace, where the scarcity of a treat becomes a source of playful tension or deception. This cultural reference is essential to understand the joke.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_841e3111",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "police_department_culture",
      "label": "Police department culture and camaraderie",
      "description": "The image and text imply a specific cultural context within police departments — one that values informal bonding, shared humor, and subtle mischief among partners or colleagues. This is a real-world phenomenon where officers often develop close-knit relationships through shared experiences, and small acts of playful deception are part of their social dynamic. Understanding this cultural context is necessary to appreciate the humor.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_977747ce",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "informal_language_use",
      "label": "Use of informal, colloquial language ('ya')",
      "description": "The phrase 'ya partner' uses informal, colloquial language that is common in casual, friendly, or regional American English. This linguistic choice signals familiarity and camaraderie between the two officers, reinforcing the playful and non-formal tone of the meme. Understanding this linguistic nuance helps interpret the tone and intent of the interaction.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_0d72fa41",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "shared_mischievous_deception",
      "label": "Shared mischievous deception as humor",
      "description": "The humor of the meme hinges on the concept of shared deception — where two people conspire to pretend something is true (no donuts left) when it’s actually false (there are two). This is a common comedic device in everyday life and media, often used to create relatable, lighthearted moments. The meme’s effectiveness depends on the audience recognizing this type of social interaction as humorous.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d69cd04f",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "mischievousness",
      "label": "the mischievous expression",
      "description": "The officers are smiling and looking at each other, suggesting they are sharing a playful, deceptive joke — pretending there are no donuts left when there are actually two. Their body language (leaning, crossed arms, direct eye contact) and the text overlay reinforce a lighthearted, conspiratorial mood.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7472ee36",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "The officers are mapped as 'you and ya partner' in the context of the joke",
      "description": "The top text 'when you and ya partner think' explicitly refers to the two police officers in the image. The visual material (police_officer_1 and police_officer_2) is mapped onto the textual entity 'you and ya partner', establishing an analogical mapping where the officers are the subjects of the scenario described in the text. Their smiling, conspiratorial interaction visually confirms they are the 'you and ya partner' engaging in the playful deception about the donuts.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_73045256",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the officers as 'you and ya partner'",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the two police officers in the image, who are explicitly referred to as 'you and ya partner' in the top text. The visual depiction of the officers smiling and looking at each other, along with the textual context, invites the viewer to identify with them as the subjects of the scenario — the two colleagues engaging in playful deception about the donuts. This projection is reinforced by the informal language 'ya' and the shared, conspiratorial body language, which signals familiarity and camaraderie. The user is thus invited to imagine themselves in the officers' shoes, experiencing the humorous moment of pretending there are no donuts left when there are actually two.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_6a0d3f1d",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "workplace_banter",
      "label": "Workplace banter and camaraderie",
      "description": "The meme targets individuals who recognize and appreciate the cultural phenomenon of playful, mischievous banter among colleagues, particularly in close-knit professional environments. The humor relies on the shared experience of workplace camaraderie and small deceptions, making it relatable to anyone who has participated in or observed such interactions.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_a46381b1"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
